THE PAPER. 18 pages. Poisson collision laws for survivor prefixes, phase resolved.

Let Y be a mixing one-step subshift of finite type carrying the equilibrium Markov measure of a one-step potential, and let H be a Markov hole whose safe graph is irreducible of period d. Take independent prefixes conditioned on survival to depth m. If the safe graph has at least two states and is not a deterministic cycle, then for every fixed k >= 2 the number of k-fold collisions has a Poisson limit at its pressure-determined birthday scale. The result is phase resolved: the Renyi power sum carries an explicit Perron prefactor depending on m-1 mod d, so the critical Poisson mean can oscillate between depth classes. The paper gives an explicit total-variation error bound and a period-two example in which the two limiting means differ, showing that a phase-free extension of the primitive-survivor collision law is in general false. I verified that example myself: the two constants are attained to full precision at every depth, because the killed matrix has spectrum plus and minus rho together with zero, so there is no error term at all in that example.

